Explanation of the new alternations:

  •  `\\\d{1,3}`       → octal escapes (`?\123`)  
  •  `\\x[A-Fa-f0-9]{1,2}` → hex escapes (`?\xA1`)  
  •  `\\u\{[A-Fa-f0-9]+\}` → Ruby’s curly‐braced Unicode (`?\u{1AF9}`)  
  •  `\\u[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}`   → four-digit Unicode (`?\uAF09`)  
  •  `(?:\\[CM]-)+\\?[^\s]` → chained control/meta (`?\C-a`, `?\M-\C-a`, etc.)  
  •  `\\\S`                → backslash plus any single non‐space (e.g. `?\n`, `?\\`)  
  •  `\S`                  → any single non‐space (e.g. `?a`, `?/`, `?あ`)  

Finally, the `(?!\S)` lookahead ensures we stop the literal exactly after that single character/escape. This update brings the Ruby mode into line with the full set of character literal forms.
